# 4. Hades: Enemies and level design

Bosses receive most of the attention, but regular encounters are just as important to the overall combat experience.

The game's main regions introduce different visual environments and enemy combinations.

## 4.1 Tartarus

Tartarus is the opening region.

It teaches the player how to move, attack, dodge, choose rewards and interact with environmental hazards.

The early encounters are comparatively straightforward, but they gradually introduce enemies that require more careful positioning.

The region's role is onboarding: the player learns the fundamentals without being confronted with the full complexity of the game immediately.

## 4.2 Asphodel

Asphodel introduces a more hazardous environment.

Its lava-covered areas make positioning more consequential. Moving away from one enemy can put Zagreus in danger if the player fails to account for the surrounding terrain.

This adds a second layer to combat.

You are no longer thinking only about where enemies are; you're also thinking about where it is safe to stand.

## 4.3 Elysium

Elysium introduces more demanding enemies and defensive mechanics.

Some opponents are harder to finish off than their basic health bars might suggest, and the region's combat encounters punish careless aggression.

This is where the player must make better use of the systems learned earlier.

A powerful weapon is helpful, but understanding which enemy to target first becomes increasingly important.

## 4.4 Temple of Styx

The Temple of Styx changes the structure of progression.

Instead of simply moving through a straightforward sequence of rooms, Zagreus explores a more branching area with multiple chambers and rewards.

The player must weigh the value of further exploration against the risk of entering additional encounters.

This creates a strategic decision: is the potential reward worth the health and resources you might lose?

### Overall level-design assessment

The regions serve different mechanical purposes:

| Region | Main design function |
|---|---|
| Tartarus | Teach the fundamentals |
| Asphodel | Add environmental hazards |
| Elysium | Increase enemy complexity and pressure |
| Temple of Styx | Introduce branching progression and risk–reward decisions |

The progression is effective because the difficulty increases through more than raw enemy statistics. It changes the situations in which the player must use their existing abilities.

