# 5. Hades: Progression, upgrades and replayability

One of the game's strongest features is that **a failed run still contributes to your overall progress**.

This is the difference between a roguelite that feels rewarding and one that feels like repeatedly starting over.

## 5.1 The House of Hades



The House of Hades acts as the central hub between escape attempts.

Here, Zagreus can:
- Speak with family members and other characters.
- Unlock permanent combat improvements.
- Change weapons.
- Spend accumulated resources.
- Develop relationships.
- Discover new dialogue and story events.
- Prepare for another run.

The hub gives the game a sense of continuity. Even when an escape attempt ends, the player returns to a place that has its own characters, atmosphere and ongoing story.

## 5.2 The Mirror of Night

The Mirror of Night provides permanent character upgrades.

It uses resources collected during runs to improve Zagreus's capabilities.

Depending on the selected talent, upgrades can affect survivability, damage, resources or other combat advantages.

The Mirror also includes alternative talent choices, allowing players to customise their preferred progression rather than simply unlocking a single linear list.

### Why it matters

Suppose a player repeatedly struggles with a boss.

They can improve their combat technique, but they can also invest in permanent upgrades that make future attempts more forgiving.

This creates two parallel forms of progression:

**Player progression:** You learn attack patterns, movement and build optimisation.

**Character progression:** Zagreus gains permanent advantages through the Mirror and other unlock systems.

These systems complement one another. Better equipment doesn't eliminate the need to learn, but learning becomes more rewarding because your character also develops.

## 5.3 Keepsakes and Companions

Keepsakes provide additional effects that influence your run.

They can be used to encourage a particular strategy or improve the consistency of your build.

Companions, unlocked through progression and relationships, provide additional abilities that can be activated in combat.

These systems reward familiarity with the cast. Characters aren't simply sources of dialogue; interactions with them can also influence gameplay.

## 5.4 Relationships and narrative progression

Hades features a large, fully voiced cast.

Relationships develop through repeated interactions, gifts and story events.

As the player progresses, new conversations reveal more about the gods, the House of Hades and Zagreus's family.

Importantly, the story isn't isolated in a separate campaign that ends when you finish the main objective. It is interwoven with the repeated escape structure.

A failed run can lead to a conversation that changes your understanding of a character. A successful run can unlock further developments.

This gives players a reason to return even when they're no longer struggling to defeat enemies.

## 5.5 The Pact of Punishment

Once unlocked through progression, the Pact of Punishment allows players to increase the difficulty of future runs.

Its conditions can affect enemy damage, enemy durability, resource availability and the structure of certain encounters.

The system is valuable because it lets experienced players customise the challenge.

A player who finds the standard game too easy can introduce more demanding conditions. Another player can leave those options alone and focus on the story.

This is a more flexible approach than forcing every player through a single escalating difficulty curve. [teemo.dev](https://teemo.dev/game-design/hades/design/overview-notes/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

