# 7. Hades: Music, sound design and voice acting



## 7.1 Soundtrack

The soundtrack was composed by Darren Korb.

Its musical identity blends rock-oriented instrumentation, rhythmic electronic elements and influences associated with Greek and Mediterranean musical traditions.

The music changes with the situation, helping distinguish ordinary exploration from more intense encounters.

Combat tracks create momentum, while quieter scenes provide room for dialogue and character development.

The soundtrack is not merely background decoration. Its musical themes help establish the game's identity and reinforce the emotional progression of the story. [University of California Press](https://online.ucpress.edu/jsmg/article-abstract/6/1/106/205401/There-Is-No-Escape-Hades-and-the-Apotheosis-of?redirectedFrom=fulltext\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 7.2 Sound effects

Combat sound effects communicate impact, movement and danger.

Attacks have distinct audio feedback, and the sounds associated with abilities help distinguish different combat events.

This becomes particularly useful when multiple effects appear on screen.

The player can use both visual and audio cues to understand what is happening.

## 7.3 Voice acting

Hades features an extensively voiced cast.

The performances give characters distinctive personalities, from Hades's authority to Zagreus's sarcasm and the gods' often dramatic self-confidence.

Repeated interactions also help the characters feel like people with ongoing relationships rather than quest dispensers.

The voice acting is especially important because the game's story unfolds in small pieces across many runs.

A conversation might last only a short time, but it can establish a relationship or reveal a new detail about the world.

### Audio verdict

Hades's sound design supports its central strengths: fast combat, recognisable characters and a story that continues between runs.

The music, voice acting and combat feedback form a cohesive presentation rather than existing as separate features.

