# 3. Which game has better boss fights?

Both games have memorable bosses, but their approaches differ.

### Hades

The original's bosses are particularly effective at testing the fundamentals of combat.

Megaera tests your ability to recognise attacks and dodge at the correct time.

The Bone Hydra introduces multiple threats and environmental hazards.

Theseus and Asterius demand careful target selection and spatial awareness.

Hades himself combines several of these challenges in the final encounter.

The boss progression feels like a sequence of increasingly demanding tests.

### Hades II

The sequel places greater emphasis on variety in the encounters and the situations surrounding them.

Hecate introduces the new combat system.

Scylla and the Sirens demand attention to multiple opponents.

Cerberus provides a different type of large-enemy encounter.

Chronos acts as the central confrontation on the Underworld route.

The surface route introduces additional bosses and settings, expanding the range of encounters available.

### Verdict

**Hades II has the broader encounter variety, while Hades has an exceptionally cohesive progression through its main boss fights.**

Neither game wins every individual encounter. The answer depends on whether you value the progression and clarity of the original or the greater variety of the sequel.

