# 8. Difficulty and replayability

Neither game is designed to be completed once and then abandoned.

Both use procedural variation, temporary upgrades and permanent progression to make repeated attempts worthwhile.

## Hades

Replayability comes from:
- Six weapon families.
- Different weapon Aspects.
- Randomised Boon combinations.
- Permanent upgrades.
- Character relationships and story events.
- Optional difficulty modifiers.
- Additional challenges for experienced players.

The result is a game in which you can revisit familiar content with a different weapon, build or difficulty setting.

## Hades II

Replayability builds on the same foundation.

It adds:
- Six Nocturnal Arms.
- Different weapon Aspects.
- Expanded magical combat.
- Additional progression systems.
- Two main routes through the world.
- More characters and encounters.
- Further difficulty customisation.

The sequel provides more systems through which runs can differ, but the experience still depends on whether you enjoy the underlying roguelite structure.

### Replayability verdict

Hades II offers more mechanical and geographical variety.

However, both games rely on repeated runs. If you dislike learning through repetition, neither will completely eliminate that issue.

